            "text": "Have you ever wondered why it’s freezing cold and snowy in January, but hot and sunny in July? A lot of people think it’s because the Earth gets closer to the sun in the summer and farther away in the winter. But guess what? That’s actually a myth! \n\nThe real reason we have seasons is all about a **tilt**. \n\nImagine the Earth is like a giant spinning top. As it spins, it travels in a big circle around the sun, which takes a whole year. But the Earth doesn’t spin straight up and down. It is tilted slightly to the side, like a toy globe sitting on your teacher's desk.\n\nBecause the Earth is tilted, different parts of the planet lean toward the sun at different times of the year:\n\n* **Summer:** When the part of the Earth where you live tilts *toward* the sun, the sunbeams shine straight down on you. The days are longer, the sun feels super strong, and everything heats up. That’s summer!\n* **Winter:** Half a year later, as the Earth moves to the other side of the sun, your part of the planet is now tilted *away* from the sun. The sunbeams hit you at a slant, so the heat spreads out and feels weaker. The days get shorter, the nights get longer, and the air gets chilly. That’s winter!\n* **Spring and Fall:** These are the in-between seasons. The Earth isn't tilted toward or away from the sun, so the weather is nice and mild. \n\nSo, next time you are building a snowman or splashing in a pool, you can thank the Earth's tilt for all the fun changes throughout the year!\n\n***\n\nP.S. Because of the tilt, when it is winter where we live, the people on the bottom half of the Earth (like in Australia) are actually enjoying the middle of summer and swimming at the beach on Christmas!",
