—predominantly women, children, and the elderly—was subjected to forced deportations into the Syrian Desert (most notably toward the concentration camp at Deir ez-Zor). Deportees were subjected to extreme heat, thirst, starvation, and regular attacks by Ottoman forces, the \"Special Organization\" (a state-run paramilitary group composed largely of released convicts), and local irregulars.\n* **Direct massacres:** In many towns and villages across Eastern Anatolia, large groups of Armenians were trapped in churches, caves, or boats and burned alive, drowned, or shot en masse before any deportation could occur.\n\n### 3. Other Affected Minorities\nThe mass killings extended beyond the Armenian population. Other Christian minorities within the Ottoman Empire were targeted in coordinated campaigns around the same time:\n* **Assyrians (Sayfo):** An estimated 150,000 to 300,000 Assyrian Christians were killed.\n* **Greeks:** Between 300,000 and 750,000 Pontic and Anatolian Greeks died during deportations and massacres between 1914 and 1922.\n\n### 4. Cultural and Material Scale\nThe scale extended beyond the loss of human life to cultural erasure:\n* **Cultural destruction:** Thousands of Armenian cultural and religious heritage sites, including ancient monasteries, churches, schools, and libraries, were looted, demolished, or repurposed.\n* **Confiscation of property:** Under the \"Abandoned Properties\" laws, homes, businesses, farms, bank accounts, and personal belongings were seized by the Ottoman state and redistributed to Muslim refugees fleeing the Balkans and Caucasus.\n* **Forced assimilation:** Tens of thousands of Armenian children were separated from their families and placed in state orphanages or Muslim households to be forcibly converted to Islam and Turkified.\n\n### 5. Historical Significance\nThe sheer scale of the systematic extermination of the Ottoman Armenians led legal scholar Raphael Lemkin to coin the word **\"genocide\"** in 1944. Lemkin specifi